WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.543
A new opinion piece in The New York

00:00:01.543 --> 00:00:02.627
Times speculates

00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:03.461
that Taylor Swift

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:06.172
may be a closeted queer person.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.507
And in the piece, editor

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.342
Anna Marks points to possible

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:13.179
LGBT Q A Plus references

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:14.264
in Taylor Swift's

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:15.640
songs and performances

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.559
as a signal of queerness.

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:19.477
However, Swift has said in the past

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:21.104
that she does not identify

00:00:21.104 --> 00:00:22.605
with that community.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.816
Some have found it unusual

00:00:24.816 --> 00:00:27.068
that The New York Times would publish

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:29.487
such an article.

00:00:29.487 --> 00:00:31.740
CNN's Oliver Darcy joins us

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.491
live with more details on this.

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:34.034
Oliver,

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:34.325
I mean,

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:36.703
how unusual is it for The New York Times

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.496
to publish this piece?

00:00:38.496 --> 00:00:38.621
I mean,

00:00:38.621 --> 00:00:40.540
it's something that you wrote about

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.459
in analyzing

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:45.712
why The New York Times went here.

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:46.921
It's very unusual.

00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:47.255
I mean,

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:49.716
you do not see major news organizations

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.134
like The New York Times

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:52.385
publishing lengthy

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:52.594
you know,

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:55.221
this was a 5000 word opinion piece

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.432
questioning the sexuality of someone

00:00:57.432 --> 00:00:58.600
known, nevertheless,

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.392
the sexuality

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:00.602
of someone like Taylor Swift,

00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:03.271
a prominent, obviously cultural force.

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:04.606
But that's what they did this week.

00:01:04.606 --> 00:01:05.774
They published the story

00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:07.067
in the opinion section

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.485
questioning her sexuality,

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:09.944
even though Taylor Swift in the past

00:01:09.944 --> 00:01:11.112
has come out and said

00:01:11.112 --> 00:01:12.614
that she is a straight

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:16.284
ally of the LGBTQ community, that she is

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:17.744
just trying to show support

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.496
as they come under attack

00:01:19.496 --> 00:01:21.081
in recent years.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.540
And so that's what's led

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:23.875
to a lot of

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:25.293
criticism of The New York Times,

00:01:25.293 --> 00:01:26.294
including from

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:28.088
some of the singer's associates.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:31.007
I've learned that they are not pleased,

00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:31.800
to say the least,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.426
with The New York Times.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:36.429
One of the associates telling me, quote,

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.264
Because of her massive success

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.516
in this moment,

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:40.809
there is a Taylor Swift

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:43.478
shaped hole in people's ethics.

00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:45.188
This article would have been allowed

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:47.023
to be written about Shawn Mendes

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:48.608
or any male artist with sexual,

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:50.193
and he has been questioned by fans.

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:51.945
There seems to be no boundary

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:53.863
some journalists won't cross

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:55.323
when writing about Taylor,

00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:57.367
regardless of how invasive

00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:00.161
untrue and inappropriate it is.

00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:01.663
All under the protective

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:03.623
veil of an opinion piece.

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.500
That's from one of the people

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.335
close to the situation

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:09.337
over in Taylor Swift's camp.

00:02:09.337 --> 00:02:10.672
Now, The New York

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:12.132
Times is not commenting.

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:14.175
They're not directly responding

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:15.343
to some of this criticism,

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:17.178
but they have pointed to

00:02:17.178 --> 00:02:18.012
what Anna Marks,

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:19.681
the editor who wrote this piece,

00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:22.600
actually wrote in her in her in her piece

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.893
preemptively addressing

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:26.020
some of the criticism Marks wrote.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:26.604
I quote,

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:28.523
I know that discussing the potential

00:02:28.523 --> 00:02:29.732
of a star's queerness

00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:31.693
before a formal declaration of identity

00:02:31.693 --> 00:02:33.069
feels to some too

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:34.571
salacious and gossip

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:36.990
fueled to be worthy of discussion.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.867
I share many of those reservations,

00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:39.659
Marx wrote.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.202
But the stories that dominate

00:02:41.202 --> 00:02:42.579
our collective imagination shape

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.289
what our culture permits artists

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:46.040
and their audiences to say and be.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.001
Every time an artist signals queerness

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:50.128
and that transmission falls on deaf ears.

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:51.963
That silent signal dies,

00:02:51.963 --> 00:02:53.590
and it goes on to say, Recognizing

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:54.924
the possibility of queerness

00:02:54.924 --> 00:02:56.384
while being conscious of the difference

00:02:56.384 --> 00:02:57.552
between possibility

00:02:57.552 --> 00:03:00.513
and certainty keeps that signal alive.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:01.931
Now, obviously,

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:03.016
associates close to Taylor

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.976
Swift don't feel that's justification

00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:06.769
for publishing this lengthy piece.

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:08.688
And now it appears there's some bad blood

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:09.606
between them

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:11.733
and the paper of record Fred.

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:12.859
Well, The New York Times,

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:13.776
as you mentioned, Wiley

00:03:13.776 --> 00:03:15.195
and a marxist responded,

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.613
The New York Times

00:03:16.613 --> 00:03:18.198
almost has to, doesn't it,

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.992
Because there is criteria

00:03:20.992 --> 00:03:24.370
measured as to what should be published.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.206
I mean, this is The New York Times,

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:26.831
after all.

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:27.665
So there has to be

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:30.460
that kind of explanation as to why.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.628
How beneficial

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.630
what what's the objective here?

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.799
And then for the writer as well.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:39.552
The question still remains, why?

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.721
What does this mean and what's the

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:45.892
what's the objective here in publishing

00:03:45.975 --> 00:03:47.685
something like this?

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:47.852
Yeah.

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:48.686
And the Times right now,

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:50.063
I mean, they're staying silent.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.314
They have not addressed this.

00:03:51.314 --> 00:03:53.274
You're not seeing an editorial leader

00:03:53.274 --> 00:03:55.068
like the head of the opinion section

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:56.486
come out and justify

00:03:56.486 --> 00:03:57.237
why they felt

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:58.655
they needed to publish the story

00:03:58.655 --> 00:03:59.239
or why they felt

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:02.033
it was newsworthy enough to really break

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:03.826
general rules and questioning

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:05.328
someone's sexuality

00:04:05.328 --> 00:04:07.121
and speculating about it

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:08.164
in The New York Times.

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:08.957
You know, The New York Times

00:04:08.957 --> 00:04:10.124
is the paper of record.

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:10.917
And so

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:12.794
usually their standards are a lot higher

00:04:12.794 --> 00:04:14.379
than other news organizations.

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:15.463
In this case, they did something

00:04:15.463 --> 00:04:17.882
most news organizations would not do,

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:19.133
but they're not defending it.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:20.802
They're just pointing right now

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:21.970
to what Marx wrote,

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.554
what I just read to you

00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:25.348
defending the reason

00:04:25.348 --> 00:04:27.141
for publishing that story.

00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:27.517
Of course,

00:04:27.517 --> 00:04:28.726
it will be intriguing

00:04:28.726 --> 00:04:29.435
in the days ahead

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:30.311
whether they do feel

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:32.981
the need to respond more directly,

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:33.648
especially now

00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:34.983
that they're fielding criticism

00:04:34.983 --> 00:04:37.277
from Taylor Swift's associates.

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:38.820
And

00:04:38.903 --> 00:04:40.029
I mean, I've been reading it.

00:04:40.029 --> 00:04:44.659
I'm just I'm trying to figure out

00:04:44.742 --> 00:04:46.869
what the meaning is here,

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:51.291
because it does seem like it's it's mean.

00:04:51.374 --> 00:04:57.255
I mean, it just seems like there there is

00:04:57.338 --> 00:05:00.591
an objective to undermine or,

00:05:00.842 --> 00:05:03.678
you know, even using

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:06.055
what it is to this association

00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:09.851
as a criticism, as as demeaning.

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:11.060
And that just seems like

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.353
that would be met

00:05:12.353 --> 00:05:15.023
with a whole host of emotions as well.

00:05:15.023 --> 00:05:15.189
Yeah.

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:16.482
And you're not the only one

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.401
that's read it and come away with that.

00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:19.152
You know,

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:21.904
The Times has been receiving criticism

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:23.948
for this piece

00:05:23.948 --> 00:05:26.743
just just exactly because of that.

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:28.578
And again, it'll be interesting

00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:28.870
to see

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.247
whether they do respond

00:05:31.247 --> 00:05:33.124
to some of those criticism.

00:05:33.124 --> 00:05:36.044
It's not every day that you have people

00:05:36.044 --> 00:05:39.005
in the orbit of someone like Taylor Swift

00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:41.758
criticizing the Times so, so sharply.

00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:42.759
Hmm. All right.

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:43.926
Oliver Darcy,

00:05:43.926 --> 00:05:45.553
let us know when you hear more.

00:05:45.553 --> 00:05:46.346
Thank you so much.

